    "created_at": "2023-04-17T05:05:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-04-17T05:05:41Z",
    "body": "I think the right starting point for any spec here is https://wicg.github.io/close-watcher/#close-signals , which we could move into HTML at any time. (And get more review on it, as part of that move.)\r\n\r\nSome notable choices made in that spec, which we could discuss:\r\n\r\n- Un-fullscreening is always top priority, and doing so stops any key events from being fired. This matches browser behavior in the tests that I ran.\r\n- The exact choice of what event triggers a close signal is left up to the user agent. E.g. <kbd>Esc</kbd> `keydown` on desktops, but maybe a swipe-up on iOS, back gesture on Android, etc.\r\n- User agents are allowed to let canceling the related event prevent the close signal. (E.g., on desktop, canceling the `keydown` event prevents the close signal.)\r\n- After that, close watchers react in a stack-like manner: most-recently-pushed triggers first.\r\n  - Currently in that document, there are two types of close watchers: `<dialog>`, and the new `CloseWatcher` API.\r\n  - If we moved the spec into HTML, we could start with `<dialog>` + `popover=\"\"`. In a separate future PR we could talk about adding `CloseWatcher`.\r\n- Close watchers in that document have some complicated stuff around user activation, to prevent you from creating tons of close watchers with no user activation, and then using this to back-trap the user. See some discussion at https://github.com/WICG/close-watcher#asking-for-confirmation and following sections. This might be controversial and could be left to a follow-up PR. (Although the latest use counters from Chromium imply that it should be easy to ship with no compat issues.)",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
